        baseOut = uint128(bound(baseOut, 0, 900));
        Coffer fresh = new Coffer(1000, 1000, 1e18);
        fresh.credit(col);
        if (baseOut > 0) {
            fresh.trade(0, baseOut);
        }
        uint256 cap = uint256(col) * fresh.refQuote() / 1e18;
        try fresh.takeLoan(cap + 1) {
            assertLe(fresh.loanOf(address(this)), cap);
        } catch {}
    }
}

The vulnerability is price-manipulation in `takeLoan`. It uses `instantPrice()` which is derived from `quoteAmt / poolBase` and can be inflated by calling `trade(0, baseOut)` to drain `poolBase`. This lets an attacker borrow more than the manipulation-resistant reference price `refQuote()` allows, breaking the invariant `loan <= collateral * refQuote / 1e18`.

**Fix:** Use `refQuote()` (oracleMark) instead of `instantPrice()` for collateral valuation.

**PoC:** Deposit 100 collateral, trade 800 base out to raise instant price from 1e18 to 5e18, then borrow `capRef+1` where `capRef` is based on `refQuote`. On vulnerable code this succeeds and loan > ref cap; after patch it reverts with `undercollateralized`.

Patched `takeLoan`:
```solidity
function takeLoan(uint256 amount) external {
    uint256 price = refQuote();
    uint256 maxDebt = collateralOf[msg.sender] * price / 1e18;
```

